  "plannerPrescription": "Build a five-round dock loading run for a 9-year-old. A fixed pod count must be dragged into a rectangle that exactly fits each arriving boat's hold — no gaps, no leftovers, no stacking outside the outline. Round 1: 24 pods, hold is 3 wide, so the child must find 3 × 8. Round 2: same 24 pods, hold is 12 wide, forcing a second decomposition of a product they already 'solved'. Round 3: 36 pods and the boat announces only its depth (4 rows) — the child must work out the width, an unknown-factor solve where the unknown is the group count. Round 4: 14 pods and the harbourmaster asks for a hold that is 4 wide; it genuinely cannot be done, and the child must say so and pick a legal hold instead — this is the direct probe of whether 'a product has one shape' has become 'a product has whichever shapes divide it'. Round 5: an unseen story — 'the lantern barge takes rows of 5 and it must be at least 6 rows deep; load 40 lanterns' — read the constraint, build 5 × 8, and defend why 8 rows and not 4. Between rounds a boat horn sounds and the tide gauge drops one notch. Speak plainly, no baby talk, no confetti for merely finishing a drag. Assignment: Festival Team Challenge. Academic construct to preserve: Lab Learner reliably transfers equal-groups, missing-factor, and word-problem interpretation on practiced material, but holds a uniqueness misconception: a product is treated as having a single valid group structure. Prediction: on unseen composite products Lab Learner will build the first correct rectangle quickly and then stall or repeat it when a different arrangement is demanded, and will attempt an impossible arrangement rather than declare it impossible. This node must test unseen transfer: change the context while preserving the construct. Author fresh items. These have already been seen and must not be reused verbatim: math.multiplication.equal_groups, math.multiplication.missing_factor, math.multiplication.word_problem_interpretation, math.multiplication.factor_pairs. Planner decision behind this node: Baseline accuracy was 0.75, inside the preregistered 0.70–1.00 band, and all four readings were unassisted — but every item was previously_practiced with provenance 'practice', so this supports the transfer hypothesis only as a warrant to test it, never as proof. The single miss landed exactly on the predicted error pattern: on math.multiplication.factor_pairs Lab Learner answered 'only one pair', treating a product as having a unique decomposition. That is a scoped misconception, not a collapse of multiplicative reasoning, so the hypothesis is revised rather than falsified: equal-groups, missing-factor, and word-problem reasoning hold, while the belief that a product names one and only one group-structure does not. Quest eligibility is already met (4 target-aligned, 3 independent correct), so the right move is an unseen-transfer Quest built so that the uniqueness belief is the thing that fails first and visibly. Created at: 2026-08-08T16:13:56.012Z.",
  "evidenceContract": {
    "academic": true,
    "engagement": true,
    "companionObservations": true
  }
}

